      "text_raw": "The most non-ideal feed-\nback circuit that can be\nfound, is probably the one\nusing a single transistor\nonly. This is a case of severe\ninput loading AND output\nloading. Everything will\ninteract with everything.\nIt is clear that in this\ncase, the feedback equations\ncan hardly be applied. An\nattempt is made in this slide.\nSome approximate expres-\nsions are given. The only\nsure way to obtain an accu-\nrate result is to substitute\nthe transistor by its equivalent circuit (with parameters g and r at low frequencies), and solve\nm o\nthe Kirchoff equations.\nAs a result this is one of the most complicated feedback circuits around, despite its simple\ncircuit configuration.",
      "text": "The most non-ideal feedback circuit that can be found, is probably the one using a single transistor only. This is a case of severe input loading AND output loading. Everything will interact with everything. It is clear that in this case, the feedback equations can hardly be applied. An attempt is made in this slide. Some approximate expressions are given. The only sure way to obtain an accurate result is to substitute the transistor by its equivalent circuit (with parameters g and r at low frequencies), and solve m o the Kirchoff equations. As a result this is one of the most complicated feedback circuits around, despite its simple circuit configuration."
    }
